PR #613  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Non-mandated CLIC trap stack layout allows handler-code to mis-identify saved-epc offset, corrupting xpistatus/mepc and enabling privilege escalation or infinite re-execution.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/ptrace.h:15-56  pt_regs
      struct pt_regs defines the canonical trap-frame layout with epc at offset 0, then GPRs ra..t6, then CSRs status/badaddr/cause/orig_a0. The kernel's own handle_exception code populates this struct via explicit CSR reads (csrr s2, CSR_EPC; REG_S s2, PT_EPC(sp)). This is internally consistent today because a single source (the struct definition) generates all PT_* offsets via asm-offsets.c. However, there is no compile-time or runtime assertion that this layout matches any CLIC hardware pushpop frame ordering. If CLIC pushpop writes a different slot ordering (e.g. xnepc instead of epc at offset 0), every consumer of this struct silently reads/writes wrong CSR slots. The struct definition itself doesn't document this assumption.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/asm-offsets.c:96-132  asm_offsets
      This is the single point of failure: every PT_* offset macro (PT_EPC=0, PT_STATUS=248, PT_CAUSE=256, etc.) is generated from struct pt_regs via offsetof(). Every assembly trap entry/exit site consumes these constants to index into the saved trap frame. If CLIC hardware pushpop writes a frame with a different CSR ordering, ALL PT_* consumers silently access wrong slots — e.g. a CSR_STATUS write lands at the offset where CLIC stored xnepc. No BUILD_BUG_ON or runtime check validates that the pt_regs layout matches the CLIC pushpop frame format. The correctness depends entirely on the unstated invariant that CLIC pushpop (if enabled) uses the same struct pt_regs layout as the kernel.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:182-194  handle_exception
      handle_exception explicitly reads individual CSRs (CSR_STATUS, CSR_EPC, CSR_TVAL, CSR_CAUSE, CSR_SCRATCH) and stores them to specific PT_* slots (PT_STATUS, PT_EPC, PT_BADADDR, PT_CAUSE, PT_TP). Since the kernel controls both the struct pt_regs layout AND the CSR-to-slot mapping, there is no mismatch — the kernel is its own source of truth. The trap frame is populated by software, not by CLIC pushpop hardware. This code would only break if the kernel were reconfigured to use CLIC pushpop to populate the frame, and the CLIC frame layout differed from struct pt_regs. In the current codebase there is no such configuration.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:298-315  ret_from_exception
      ret_from_exception restores CSR_STATUS (line 301) and CSR_EPC (line 302) from the PT_STATUS(sp) and PT_EPC(sp) slots that handle_exception previously populated. Since both entry and exit use the same struct pt_regs layout and the frame is populated by software (not CLIC pushpop), the slot mapping is self-consistent. The dangling-LR-clearing sequence (REG_L a2, PT_EPC(sp); REG_SC x0, a2, PT_EPC(sp)) at lines 298-299 correctly targets the epc slot. No mismatch risk exists under the current software-driven trap entry model.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:327-334  do_trap_ecall_u
      At line 332: regs->epc += 4 advances the saved EPC by one 4-byte instruction. This writes to offset 0 of struct pt_regs (the 'epc' field). The code assumes that offset 0 of the trap frame always contains epc and only epc. Under the current software-driven trap entry (handle_exception stores CSR_EPC to PT_EPC at offset 0), this is correct. However, if CLIC pushpop hardware placed a different CSR (e.g. xnepc or xpistatus) at offset 0, this += 4 would corrupt a privilege/interrupt-status CSR instead of advancing mepc — enabling infinite ECALL loops or privilege-state corruption. This is the highest-impact location in the analysis because it's the most frequently invoked trap handler.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_sbi.c:587-650  kvm_riscv_vcpu_sbi_ecall
      At line 644: cp->sepc += 4 advances the guest SEPC by one instruction. This writes to the sepc field of kvm_cpu_context. The KVM guest context (kvm_cpu_context) is a software-defined struct with its own sepc/sstatus field ordering, populated by explicit CSR reads in SAVE_GUEST_AND_RESTORE_HOST_CSRS (csrr t3, CSR_SEPC; REG_S t3, KVM_ARCH_GUEST_SEPC(a0)). This is internally consistent because KVM controls both the struct layout and the CSR save code. However, the conditionally-skipped advance at line 643-644 depends on the `next_sepc` boolean, and in the utrap-redirect path (line 628-629), sepc is overwritten from the utrap struct BEFORE the conditional += 4 can fire (since next_sepc is set to false). The logic is correct today but depends on the unstated invariant that the kvm_cpu_context.sepc field corresponds to the same architectural register as what hardware would write in a CLIC pushpop scenario.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_switch.S:227-248  __kvm_riscv_nacl_switch_to
      The nested acceleration context switch saves host GPRs/CSRs using KVM_ARCH_HOST_*/KVM_ARCH_GUEST_* offsets (derived from kvm_cpu_context struct), then issues a raw ECALL at line 235. Two nested levels of trap-frame layout assumptions exist here: (1) the outer switch macros (SAVE_HOST_GPRS, SAVE_HOST_AND_RESTORE_GUEST_CSRS) assume kvm_cpu_context layout matches the host trap frame, and (2) the inner ECALL at line 235 causes the guest OS's own trap handler to read/write the guest's pt_regs layout. Both levels must agree with any CLIC pushpop frame format. The ECALL at line 235 also means CLIC pushpop (if enabled on the guest hart) would write a hardware frame that the guest OS handler then interprets using its struct pt_regs — a third level of layout assumption not visible in this code.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_switch.S:250-271  __kvm_riscv_unpriv_trap
