  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_switch.S:198-219  __kvm_riscv_switch_to
      The main KVM vCPU context switch saves/restores host GPRs/CSRs using KVM_ARCH_HOST_* and KVM_ARCH_GUEST_* offsets, then issues sret at line 206. The host-to-guest transition via sret loads guest SEPC/SSTATUS from the guest_context struct and writes them to hardware CSRs before sret. On the return path (line 211-215), the guest CSR state is captured back into guest_context. All these offsets are generated from the kvm_cpu_context struct layout. If CLIC pushpop hardware writes frames with a different CSR ordering, both the host-save and guest-restore macros access wrong fields within the kvm_cpu_context — potentially swapping sepc with sstatus. No runtime validation guards against this.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/ptrace.h:72-80  instruction_pointer / instruction_pointer_set
      instruction_pointer(regs) returns regs->epc — the field at offset 0 of struct pt_regs. instruction_pointer_set() writes to the same field. These are called by stack unwinders, kprobes, signal delivery, kgdb, and the ECALL handler (via regs->epc += 4 in do_trap_ecall_u). If CLIC pushpop stores mepc at a non-zero offset within the frame, all callers get the wrong saved PC. For example, show_regs() reports wrong EPC, stack unwinding follows wrong return addresses, and signal handlers see garbage instead of the faulting PC. The inlined getter/setter provide no indirection or validation that offset 0 contains epc.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/asm.h:128-155  save_from_x6_to_x31
      Assembly macro saving x6-x31 into pt_regs slots using hardcoded PT_* offsets (e.g. REG_S x6, PT_T1(sp), REG_S x10, PT_A0(sp), etc.). The GPR-to-slot mapping assumes x6=t1 goes to offsetof(pt_regs, t1), x10=a0 goes to offsetof(pt_regs, a0), etc. Used by handle_exception, handle_kernel_stack_overflow, and arch_rethook_trampoline. If CLIC pushpop assigns different slot indices for the same architectural registers, wrong register values are saved. However, the macro is self-consistent with struct pt_regs and the PT_* offset definitions. The risk is that a CLIC pushpop frame might assign GPRs to different offsets than the kernel's struct pt_regs layout.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/asm.h:158-185  restore_from_x6_to_x31
      Mirror-image of save_from_x6_to_x31: restores x6-x31 from the same hardcoded PT_* offsets. Used by ret_from_exception. If CLIC pushpop wrote GPRs to different slots than the kernel's pt_regs layout expects, this macro loads wrong values into GPRs on return-from-exception — effectively swapping register contents across the sret/mret boundary. This could, for example, restore a user-controlled value from a data register slot into sp (x2), giving an attacker control of the kernel stack pointer after exception return.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:334-354  handle_kernel_stack_overflow
      Saves/restores context to overflow stack using the same hardcoded PT_* offsets as handle_exception and the same save_from_x6_to_x31 macro. Lines 341-351 explicitly read CSRs (CSR_STATUS, CSR_EPC, CSR_TVAL, CSR_CAUSE, CSR_SCRATCH) and store them to PT_STATUS, PT_EPC, PT_BADADDR, PT_CAUSE, PT_TP. This is self-consistent with the rest of the kernel's trap frame model. Same layout-mismatch risk as the main exception path — triggered on kernel stack overflow rather than normal exception entry. The overflow path would corrupt context differently from the normal path, making debugging harder.
